Bronx vs. Manhattan (3–5 sentences)
In November 2025, the Bronx’s top complaints were led by Noise - 
Residential (29.75%) and HEAT/HOT WATER (22.33%), 
while Manhattan’s top five were HEAT/HOT WATER (11,438; 18.46%), 
Noise - Residential (6,024; 9.72%), Illegal Parking (5,910; 9.54%), 
Noise - Street/Sidewalk (3,029; 4.89%), 
and Encampment (2,967; 4.79%) out of 61,956 total complaints. 
Compared with the Bronx, Manhattan shows a more mixed profile with relatively more street/public-space issues (street noise, encampment, illegal parking) and a smaller share concentrated in a single residential-noise category. Hypothesis: these differences may reflect differences in land use and density (more commercial/tourist and sidewalk activity in Manhattan), housing/building conditions and heating complaint patterns, and reporting behavior—but the data cannot prove causes, and as seen in the Bronx, repeated calls from specific addresses can also skew category totals.
